do pessoa whenever  someone tells me he dreamed i wonder if  he realizes that he has never done  anything but dream  the average modern american adult gets  around 7 hours of sleep per night over  the course of an average lifespan of  about 80 years that means the average  person spends around 23 years asleep or  about one-third of their lifetime  there are five stages of sleep across  two main categories non-rem and rem  sleep  starting in non-rem we move through the  first four stages where the body and  brain increasingly relax and slow down  the breath and heart rate reduce the  body's temperature decreases and brain  waves slow becoming what are known as  delta waves  this period of non-rem sleep makes up  around five hours of sleep per night or  around 75 percent of sleep in general  there are essentially no dreams during  these stages and so as far as we can  tell there is no thought or conscious  experience at all  our subjective experience of self  appears to disappear a strange almost  mystical loss of self into nothingness  and so at around 75 percent of sleep  this means that across your lifetime you  will spend around 17 years in this state  essentially equivalent to death  at stage 5 however you return and you  enter into the second category of sleep  rem sleep during this stage the brain  paralyzes the body the eyes dart back  and forth like a glitching animatronic  the heart rate and breath spike back up  and the brain returns to a near awake  state  and here is where most streams occur  throughout an average night we cycle  through the five stages about four to  six times and we spend about two hours  per night in the stream stage or around  25 percent of sleep in general  and so across your lifetime you will  live about six years  in a dream  in this other strange state of sleep  where the lines between reality and  non-reality blur and an entire seemingly  self-created realm is experienced  but what exactly is this realm what are  the implications of experiencing it  something that seems so real but isn't  why do we experience it how do we why do  we even sleep and dream at all  despite all modern science and  technology and despite how prevalent and  how much of our lives are surrendered to  these years of sleeping and dreaming  these questions remain largely  unanswered  the reasons for why we need to sleep  each night are obvious a lack of sleep  weakens our memory and ability to think  clearly it can cause mood swings and  hallucinations nerve cells may not  function well the immune system can  weaken and much worse  but the reason for why sleep is needed  for these reasons is still unclear  there are of course many theories  related to why we sleep as well as  pertaining to the functions of rem sleep  and dreaming  revolutionary psychologists sigmund  freud and carl jung directed much of  their psychoanalytic efforts toward  dreams both believing that dreams serve  to provide a relevant conscious window  into the unconscious mind freud  specifically believing that dreams were  symbolic representations of repressed  fears and desires jung believing that  they were more often symbolic  manifestations of the collective  unconscious of humanity as a whole  following the 20th century increased  understandings of the mind have also  allowed for neurobiological theories to  form like activation synthesis theory  which argues that dreams are merely a  product of random neural activity taking  place in the brain stem and in the  cerebral cortex attempting to make sense  of the meaningless activity by forming  thoughts and stories out of it  other theories suggest that dreaming  helps memory learning or forgetting  unnecessary or harmful information  dreaming either being a primary or  secondary phenomenon to these  neurobiological processes  and theories like threat simulation  theory and social simulation theory  argue that dreams serve as a sort of  practice rehearsal to improve one's  abilities and likelihood of success in  threatening events or important social  situations  these are just a few of the many  theories focused just on dreams and rem  sleep however since the mind is still  largely not understood and since  consciousness is such an unbreachable  subjective realm all theories are  essentially both unfalsifiable and  unprovable  and so there is still no real consensus  on why we dream or how dreams are  created  perhaps most confounding and unsettling  is this last part how the mind creates  dreams  how does it create a reality so real  insider sleeping unmoving head without  ever moving the body or experiencing  anything outside of it in a dream you  experience the physical sensations and  visuals of a world that feels completely  real  to your dream self it is real  and here that classic cliche trope of  not knowing if you are in a dream  becomes eerily uncertain and ripe with  reasons for literary and philosophical  exploration  typically the strange things that happen  in dreams don't seem strange when we are  in them it is only after we awake and  recollect them through what we believe  to be our normal state do we sift them  through a filter of normality and derive  their strangeness  but if things and dreams don't seem  strange when we are in them how do we  ever know that any filter through which  we determine what is normal when we seem  to be awake is not in fact also part of  a dream  when i consider this carefully 17th  century philosopher rene descartes wrote  i find not a single property which with  certainty separates the waking state  from the dream how can you be certain  that your whole life is not a dream  how do we know that we will not reawaken  into another state that will turn  everything that feels normal right now  into a memory of a strange dreamt  reality  of course this is very unlikely to be  true but for the same reasons we cannot  prove or disprove any theories of why  and how we dream because we can't  sufficiently enter the minds of others  nor escape our own we cannot truly prove  or disprove that we are not in one right  now  there are certainly things that seem to  be obvious indications of reality  perhaps things like the level of  perceptual detail the experience of pain  the pace of time and events the  consistency of memories a memory of  having woken up in the morning and so on  but many people have dreams where they  awake at the beginning of it in what is  known as a false awakening in which the  dreamer experiences waking up normally  in their bed often followed by intensely  vivid details of ordinary life but yet  they are still asleep in a dream  and moreover how do you know that your  assessment or beliefs about what is  sufficiently detailed or painful is not  a dreamt conclusion how do you know that  your memories are not dreamt how do you  know that your experience of having  awoken this morning with a memory of  having dreamt the night before although  it felt like you were remembering a  dream you are not actually remembering  reality  in truth it seems that any test or claim  of knowing you are awake could  theoretically take place in a dream  of course again this is extremely  unlikely and is perhaps more to serve as  an interesting philosophical  contemplation as opposed to a  practically useful takeaway but  nonetheless the contemplation shows us  when our reasoning for believing  something is true or real is because  it's obvious that does not necessarily  mean it is  without any concrete knowledge evidence  or way of proving it we find ourselves  on a shaky ground of knowledge and  uncertainty at the beginning and end of  each day  the good news though is that whether you  are in a dream right now or not likely  doesn't matter if you can't know the  difference then what's the difference if  love and fear and meaning is felt in  your dreams apparent truths discovered  and wonders found if experiences are  experienced in anything anywhere does it  matter if it is objectively real in the  way you think  the fact that you exist anywhere in any  way able to experience anything is all  you can and all you truly need to know  and perhaps if we wish to wake up from  our dream if we are in one we simply  need to close our eyes and find the real  world  whether this reality is a dream or not  there is endless information to learn  about it while you are still here  whether you want to learn more about the  nature of reality the science of  dreaming and sleeping the importance of  sleeping for health or anything else  this video sponsor blinkist is an  incredible tool that can help you learn  about all sorts of subjects from all  sorts of books no matter how much waking  time you have blinkist works by taking  over 5 000 of the best nonfiction books  in 27 different categories pulls out the  key takeaways and forms them into 15  minute text and audio summaries that can  be read or listened to even while  offline in doing so blinkist can help  you turn personal or otherwise unused  moments of time like your commute  errands breaks in your schedule and so  on into meaningful and enriching  opportunities to learn more about the  world and yourself enter the strange  world and concept of lucid dreaming on  blankest with the title exploring the  world of lucid dreaming by stephen  labarge and howard reingold in which the  two provide interesting insights about  lucid dreaming a state in which people  claim awareness of dreaming while in  their dreams as well as a guide to  potentially enhance the lucidity of your  own dreams and how this can potentially  contribute to your waking life  use the link in the description and  you'll receive one free week of  unlimited access as well as 25 off a  premium membership the free seven day  trial can be canceled anytime within the  trial period  and of course as always thank you so  much for watching in general if you  enjoy exploring your curiosity through  content like this then be sure to check  o